// Pull request author: swachchhanda000
// Change: new rule
// Title: Potential Tampering of Service Privileges in Registry
// Sigma ID: ad682e11-8144-4de0-aa39-1cfcd5ebb64e
// Status: experimental | Level: medium
// Author: Swachchhanda Shrawan Poudel (Nextron Systems)
// Date: 2026-09-27 | Modified: -
// ATT&CK: execution, privilege-escalation, stealth, T1574.011, T1068
// Description: Detects potentially suspicious modifications to the Windows Registry that add suspicious
//   privileges to services, potentially indicating preparation for EDR bypass or kernel driver
//   loading. These elevated privileges enable loading kernel drivers, which can bypass or disable
//   Endpoint Detection and Response (EDR) tools on the compromised system. If privileges like
//   'SeLoadDriverPrivilege' or 'SeDebugPrivilege' are added to a service, it may indicate malicious
//   intent to escalate privileges or evade security measures.
// False positives:
//   - Legitimate service configuration changes by administrators or authorized software.
//   - Some binaries may be used by installers or system management tools, therefore, use baselining to reduce false positives before pushing to production.
// References:
//   - https://blog.fox-it.com/2025/09/01/three-lazarus-rats-coming-for-your-cheese/
// Sigma rule: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/blob/e518ed5635f1cb438da9c24198ce30d826249df3/rules/windows/registry/registry_set/registry_set_potential_tampering_of_service_privileges.yml
// License: Detection Rule License (DRL) 1.1 - https://github.com/SigmaHQ/Detection-Rule-License
// Converted by sigma2kql (target: microsoft_xdr, pySigma 1.5.1, pySigma-backend-kusto 1.0.1)
// Validation: passed (Kusto.Language, Defender schema)
// Machine-generated and not tested against real telemetry. Verify before use as a detection.
// Note: 1 wildcard comparison(s) rewritten to 'matches regex' (KQL treats * in contains/endswith/== as a literal character)

DeviceRegistryEvents
| where (InitiatingProcessFolderPath endswith "\\reg.exe" or InitiatingProcessFolderPath endswith "\\powershell.exe" or InitiatingProcessFolderPath endswith "\\pwsh.exe" or InitiatingProcessFolderPath endswith "\\wscript.exe" or InitiatingProcessFolderPath endswith "\\cscript.exe") and RegistryKey matches regex @"(?i)\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Services.*$" and RegistryKey endswith "\\RequiredPrivileges" and RegistryValueData =~ "Binary Data"
